1
00:02:43,280 --> 00:02:45,191
Who's in here?

2
00:02:52,160 --> 00:02:53,559
Hey!

3
00:02:53,720 --> 00:02:55,995
Stop right there!

4
00:03:16,320 --> 00:03:19,073
Bill, we're having a lot of guests today,
so move the monitors back.

5
00:03:19,240 --> 00:03:21,310
Give them plenty of room.

6
00:03:29,680 --> 00:03:31,716
Steve, how you doing?

7
00:03:31,880 --> 00:03:33,438
- Damn.
- Problem?

8
00:03:33,600 --> 00:03:37,434
- Someone broke in here last night.
- Really? Why would anyone break in?

9
00:03:37,600 --> 00:03:39,113
Well, it's probably some viewer

10
00:03:39,280 --> 00:03:44,115
desperately seeking my recipe
for <i>blanquette de veau.</i>

11
00:03:48,080 --> 00:03:49,911
Listen,
about the guest I scheduled today.

12
00:03:50,080 --> 00:03:53,231
That darling little chef
from the Brittany?

13
00:03:53,400 --> 00:03:56,198
If you tell me he had to cancel,
you're gonna break my heart.

14
00:03:56,360 --> 00:03:58,237
He didn't cancel.
He'll be on next week.

15
00:03:58,400 --> 00:04:00,231
So who's gonna be on today?

16
00:04:01,400 --> 00:04:03,391
Well, I'm sure you've heard
that Michael's in town

17
00:04:03,560 --> 00:04:05,278
promoting his new cookbook.

18
00:04:06,320 --> 00:04:08,629
- You're not serious.
- He'll be here in 30 minutes.

19
00:04:08,840 --> 00:04:10,956
Now, I know it'll be a little awkward
for you, but--

20
00:04:11,120 --> 00:04:14,430
Awkward? Awkward?

21
00:04:16,880 --> 00:04:19,917
Victoria, like it or not,
right now Michael Edwards is hot.

22
00:04:20,080 --> 00:04:21,672
His book is going to be a bestseller.

23
00:04:21,840 --> 00:04:23,956
Everything that is in that book
I taught him

24
00:04:24,120 --> 00:04:25,394
in the 1 2 years we were married.

25
00:04:25,560 --> 00:04:29,189
Victoria, you are the last word
on cooking in Atlanta,

26
00:04:29,360 --> 00:04:32,238
but we're having a hard time
selling the show in syndication.

27
00:04:33,560 --> 00:04:38,236
We need guests like Michael Edwards
or there won't be any show.

28
00:04:39,280 --> 00:04:41,999
Michael is on the show.

29
00:04:44,840 --> 00:04:48,719
<i>You'll notice that as I cook the sauce</i>
<i>over high heat, I stir it constantly.</i>

30
00:04:48,880 --> 00:04:51,952
<i>The point is you want this sauce</i>
<i>to thicken and not burn.</i>

31
00:04:52,360 --> 00:04:55,636
Right you are again, Michael.
That was very well put.

32
00:04:55,800 --> 00:04:58,473
How's your book doing?
I hear it's just flying off the stands.

33
00:04:58,640 --> 00:05:02,155
As a matter of fact, I hear it's selling
extremely well coast to coast.

34
00:05:02,320 --> 00:05:04,675
I hear it's the result of 1 2 years
of research.

35
00:05:04,840 --> 00:05:08,435
It seemed like 1 2 centuries.

36
00:05:09,280 --> 00:05:11,350
Had we more time,
I would pour this heated sauce

37
00:05:11,520 --> 00:05:14,353
over the meat and vegetables,
stir gently,

38
00:05:14,520 --> 00:05:17,830
and then simmer until the veal
is thoroughly heated.

39
00:05:18,000 --> 00:05:22,790
But because we are almost out of time,
I'll just show you the results.

40
00:05:22,960 --> 00:05:25,428
- There it is.
- May I taste it?

41
00:05:26,040 --> 00:05:27,917
Sure.

42
00:05:39,040 --> 00:05:40,473
This is remarkably good,

43
00:05:40,640 --> 00:05:43,757
and remarkably similar
to a recipe you'll find in my book.

44
00:05:43,920 --> 00:05:46,195
What a coincidence.

45
00:05:56,240 --> 00:05:58,754
Dave, we have a problem.
Stop the tape.

46
00:06:00,800 --> 00:06:03,155
Oh, Leslie. Please, do something.

47
00:06:06,760 --> 00:06:09,115
Too late. He's dead.

48
00:06:21,080 --> 00:06:25,278
Wait, wait, wait. Now get it.
Go get it. What a fine dog.

49
00:06:25,440 --> 00:06:28,671
What a good dog. Come on.
Come on, Max.

50
00:06:28,840 --> 00:06:32,310
Come on, now. You wait outside.
Wait right here. Wait right here.

51
00:06:32,480 --> 00:06:33,708
Wait right here.

52
00:06:33,880 --> 00:06:37,350
Hello, ladies. Fine day, isn't it?

53
00:06:37,520 --> 00:06:42,674
All right. One county heard from.
Now somebody else.

54
00:06:47,160 --> 00:06:50,391
What do I need to do? Talk to me.

55
00:06:52,480 --> 00:06:54,471
You got a problem,
I need to know about it.

56
00:06:54,640 --> 00:06:58,952
Well, Daddy, just tell them how good
they'll go with dumplings.

57
00:07:00,000 --> 00:07:02,309
I don't know, Charlene.

58
00:07:03,440 --> 00:07:06,352
One day they will,
one day they won't.

59
00:07:07,000 --> 00:07:10,231
- Life's a mystery.
- So I hear.

60
00:07:11,240 --> 00:07:13,310
Watch your step.

61
00:07:13,760 --> 00:07:15,990
Years of training.

62
00:07:17,440 --> 00:07:19,158
You hear that Victoria Edwards
was arrested?

63
00:07:19,320 --> 00:07:20,639
Yeah. I saw it on the news.

64
00:07:20,800 --> 00:07:24,793
They say her ex-husband
died of eating her cooking.

65
00:07:24,960 --> 00:07:27,633
Frank Daniels had an anonymous tip
to run an autopsy,

66
00:07:27,800 --> 00:07:30,234
and there was poison
found in her ex-husband's system

67
00:07:30,400 --> 00:07:32,868
and there was a bottle of it
found in Victoria's house.

68
00:07:33,040 --> 00:07:35,793
Now, Daddy,
if I seem to know a lot about it,

69
00:07:35,960 --> 00:07:40,112
it's because we represented Victoria
at her bail hearing yesterday.

70
00:07:40,520 --> 00:07:44,638
- We represented her?
- Well, I represented her for us.

71
00:07:44,800 --> 00:07:46,870
- Are you cooking something?
- No.

72
00:07:47,040 --> 00:07:50,032
- You making corn bread?
- No.

73
00:07:50,200 --> 00:07:52,873
Somebody's making corn bread.

74
00:07:58,040 --> 00:08:00,349
- Daddy, this is Victoria Edwards.
- How do you do?

75
00:08:00,520 --> 00:08:02,511
- How do you do?
- You can take the corn bread out.

76
00:08:02,680 --> 00:08:05,638
Everything is ready. Nice and hot.

77
00:08:05,800 --> 00:08:07,518
Victoria and I got to talking
about her case

78
00:08:07,680 --> 00:08:09,352
and, well, one thing led to another.

79
00:08:09,520 --> 00:08:15,356
And then we got to talking about you
and again one thing led to another.

80
00:08:15,520 --> 00:08:18,671
And then finally it came out that
your favourite meal was corn bread,

81
00:08:18,840 --> 00:08:21,832
country-fried steak, creamed corn,
and sliced tomatoes.

82
00:08:22,480 --> 00:08:25,153
Taking your case might need
a little persuasion.

83
00:08:25,320 --> 00:08:28,392
So I thought I'd prepare
a little country repast.

84
00:08:28,640 --> 00:08:30,790
Well, Miss Victoria, two things:

85
00:08:30,960 --> 00:08:34,396
One, I'm working on
some very difficult cases right now.

86
00:08:34,560 --> 00:08:36,391
And, yes,
this is one of my favourite meals,

87
00:08:36,560 --> 00:08:38,357
and you know who used to make it.

88
00:08:38,520 --> 00:08:39,509
Your mother.

89
00:08:39,680 --> 00:08:45,915
So why don't you sit down
and try some while it's still hot.

90
00:08:47,360 --> 00:08:51,194
That smells so good, I don't think
I can even watch you eat it.

91
00:08:51,600 --> 00:08:54,478
My nutritionist has me on
a food-group diet.

92
00:08:54,640 --> 00:08:55,959
Today, it's fruit.

93
00:08:57,920 --> 00:09:01,595
I have to make a call,
cancel a luncheon date.

94
00:09:02,080 --> 00:09:03,672
Excuse me.

95
00:09:10,040 --> 00:09:11,029
What do you think?

96
00:09:12,480 --> 00:09:13,754
Tell me about your ex-husband.

97
00:09:15,000 --> 00:09:16,319
Well, when I married Michael,

98
00:09:16,480 --> 00:09:21,508
he was very attractive,
very charming.

99
00:09:21,680 --> 00:09:24,638
So I got him cooking,
and the next thing I know,

100
00:09:24,800 --> 00:09:26,756
not only is he divorcing me,

101
00:09:26,920 --> 00:09:30,435
but he's taking over the gourmet show
I started in New York.

102
00:09:30,600 --> 00:09:32,830
I wound up with a cooking school
in Atlanta.

103
00:09:33,000 --> 00:09:35,673
- You don't use much sugar, do you?
- No.

104
00:09:36,480 --> 00:09:40,109
- Did you dip the steak in batter?
- No, no. Just a little flour.

105
00:09:40,280 --> 00:09:45,400
- It's not fried in bacon grease, is it?
- No. A very light vegetable oil.

106
00:09:45,560 --> 00:09:48,711
You know,
my mother used to make this,

107
00:09:48,880 --> 00:09:52,316
and I'd eat it while I listened to
<i>The Lone Ranger</i> on the radio.

108
00:09:53,800 --> 00:09:56,189
Mama wasn't a very good cook.

109
00:09:56,360 --> 00:09:58,590
Now that I look back,
I don't know whether it was the meal

110
00:09:58,760 --> 00:10:01,638
I enjoyed so much
or <i>The Lone Ranger.</i>

111
00:10:05,840 --> 00:10:11,915
- So you gonna take the case?
- Lord, Charlene. I'm in love.

112
00:10:21,560 --> 00:10:24,154
- Good morning, people.
- Morning, Tyler. How goes it?

113
00:10:24,320 --> 00:10:27,312
Must be going pretty well, from
the look of that self-satisfied smile.

114
00:10:27,480 --> 00:10:30,597
I just won the Junior Chamber
of Commerce Chilli Cook-off.

115
00:10:30,760 --> 00:10:33,877
- Two thousand dollars.
- Two thousand dollars?

116
00:10:34,040 --> 00:10:35,553
That must be some chilli.

117
00:10:35,720 --> 00:10:37,119
A Victoria Edwards recipe:

118
00:10:37,280 --> 00:10:40,113
Chilli made with chicken
with a peanut butter sauce,

119
00:10:40,280 --> 00:10:42,999
with a few secret ingredients
of my own on the side.

120
00:10:43,160 --> 00:10:44,195
Sounds awful.

121
00:10:44,360 --> 00:10:49,036
Tyler, meet Victoria Edwards.
This is Tyler Hudson, our investigator.

122
00:10:49,200 --> 00:10:50,997
My dear lady,
it is a pleasure to meet you.

123
00:10:51,160 --> 00:10:53,799
- I rarely miss your show.
- Well, thank you.

124
00:10:53,960 --> 00:10:56,520
What'd you find out
down at the police lab?

125
00:10:56,680 --> 00:11:00,275
- Sodium cyanide.
- Sodium cyanide.

126
00:11:00,440 --> 00:11:02,954
Will kill you in between heartbeats.

127
00:11:05,760 --> 00:11:08,797
Who do you know
that wants to kill you?

128
00:11:10,000 --> 00:11:12,833
Kill me? Why do you think
it was meant for me?

129
00:11:13,000 --> 00:11:15,309
Because you taste the things
you cook on your show.

130
00:11:15,480 --> 00:11:17,630
Of course.

131
00:11:18,400 --> 00:11:19,515
And since Michael was on,

132
00:11:19,680 --> 00:11:23,275
I was instructed to feature him
as much as possible.

133
00:11:23,440 --> 00:11:25,351
I let him do all the tasting.

134
00:11:25,520 --> 00:11:26,953
And this is Atlanta.

135
00:11:27,120 --> 00:11:29,918
Michael Edwards didn't have
any enemies here, did he?

136
00:11:30,080 --> 00:11:31,433
No. I wouldn't think so.

137
00:11:31,600 --> 00:11:36,116
Well, I'll bet whoever broke in here
that night planted the poison.

138
00:11:36,280 --> 00:11:37,633
The security guard told the police

139
00:11:37,800 --> 00:11:40,553
that he saw a light come on
in here momentarily.

140
00:11:40,720 --> 00:11:42,790
And I'll bet that's the light
that comes on

141
00:11:42,960 --> 00:11:44,951
when you open the refrigerator door.

142
00:11:45,120 --> 00:11:47,429
All the ingredients
were kept in there.

143
00:11:47,600 --> 00:11:50,160
That's how someone
planted the poison.

144
00:11:50,720 --> 00:11:52,312
I'd like to go to your house
and check out

145
00:11:52,480 --> 00:11:55,552
where the police found that bottle
of sodium cyanide, but I'll need a key.

146
00:11:55,720 --> 00:11:57,631
Oh, no.
My housekeeper will let you in.

147
00:11:57,800 --> 00:12:00,758
Oh, great. Thanks. Later.

148
00:12:03,560 --> 00:12:05,278
You got any enemies?

149
00:12:06,840 --> 00:12:10,958
Well, naturally, anybody in my position
makes enemies, you know?

150
00:12:11,120 --> 00:12:13,680
People in my world take their food
pretty seriously.

151
00:12:13,840 --> 00:12:19,358
I mean, do you know anybody
that really has it in for you?

152
00:12:21,680 --> 00:12:27,277
Well, Christopher Hoyt, I suppose,
and Peter Leone, perhaps.

153
00:12:27,440 --> 00:12:29,396
Possibly Stephen Lichtman,

154
00:12:30,040 --> 00:12:32,793
but I can't imagine
they'd go so far as to--

155
00:12:35,200 --> 00:12:36,838
They were here.

156
00:12:37,000 --> 00:12:42,074
They were all here as guests
when Michael was poisoned.

157
00:12:42,240 --> 00:12:45,789
Tyler, I want you to stick close
to Miss Edwards for the time being.

158
00:12:45,960 --> 00:12:47,678
I'm here.

159
00:12:49,600 --> 00:12:51,477
Could you get that pan down
for me, please?

160
00:12:51,640 --> 00:12:53,073
I have a slight problem with heights.

161
00:12:53,240 --> 00:12:56,277
My dear lady, retrieving your pan
is my pleasure.

162
00:12:56,440 --> 00:12:58,192
Thank you.

163
00:12:59,120 --> 00:13:01,270
Lieutenant Daniels.

164
00:13:06,040 --> 00:13:08,634
Frank? Ben. You free for lunch?

165
00:13:14,040 --> 00:13:15,996
All right, I'm here.

166
00:13:22,720 --> 00:13:23,755
Are you wired?

167
00:13:24,280 --> 00:13:27,875
Yes, I am wired, but the question is
''why am I wired?''

168
00:13:28,040 --> 00:13:30,873
- I need you to help me.
- Do what?

169
00:13:33,200 --> 00:13:35,634
Find the person who killed
Michael Edwards.

170
00:13:36,240 --> 00:13:38,276
Is everything all right, Mr. Hoyt?

171
00:13:38,440 --> 00:13:42,638
The pƒt‚ is dry, the wine warm,
the vinaigrette too oily,

172
00:13:42,800 --> 00:13:45,872
the butter rock-hard, and there is
enough sand in these oysters

173
00:13:46,040 --> 00:13:48,110
to declare this beachfront property.

174
00:13:48,280 --> 00:13:50,555
You know, it seems to me
I've read your column

175
00:13:50,720 --> 00:13:53,393
in the <i>Atlanta Examiner</i> for years.

176
00:13:53,560 --> 00:13:54,754
Six.

177
00:13:55,120 --> 00:13:58,430
How do you feel about
Victoria Edwards?

178
00:13:58,880 --> 00:14:00,279
Well, actually, I am surprised

179
00:14:00,440 --> 00:14:02,670
that she hasn't poisoned more people
one way or another.

180
00:14:02,840 --> 00:14:06,833
Woman doesn't cook,
she folds, spindles and mutilates.

181
00:14:07,000 --> 00:14:10,072
I really do wish I could be of more help,
but you see, unfortunately,

182
00:14:10,240 --> 00:14:11,992
I am dedicated to telling the truth.

183
00:14:12,160 --> 00:14:13,752
Good, good, good.

184
00:14:13,920 --> 00:14:17,230
You know, I've heard that you
publicly stated

185
00:14:17,400 --> 00:14:19,391
that she damaged your reputation.

186
00:14:19,560 --> 00:14:22,120
Didn't several newspapers
drop your syndicated column?

187
00:14:22,280 --> 00:14:24,191
I believe your book deal fell through.

188
00:14:24,800 --> 00:14:30,158
Oh, well, it's true she did ridicule me
on her pathetic cooking show, but...

189
00:14:30,320 --> 00:14:33,869
Well, actually, see, really, that could
only enhance my reputation.

190
00:14:34,040 --> 00:14:35,951
You didn't try to get her
off the show?

191
00:14:36,120 --> 00:14:38,680
Well, you know,
I was rather annoyed at the time.

192
00:14:39,040 --> 00:14:43,830
Yeah, perhaps l-- I mentioned it
to the station manager.

193
00:14:44,000 --> 00:14:47,788
You know, inexplicably--
You know, I have lost my appetite.

194
00:14:48,320 --> 00:14:49,355
Cheque, please.

195
00:14:49,520 --> 00:14:51,875
Why were you at the taping
of her last show?

196
00:14:52,040 --> 00:14:55,350
Oh, well, I wanted to interview
Michael Edwards afterwards.

197
00:14:55,520 --> 00:14:57,511
You know, for my column.

198
00:14:59,080 --> 00:15:01,469
Where were you the night before
around 1 1 :00?

199
00:15:03,400 --> 00:15:06,790
You know, you really do
ask an awful lot of questions.

200
00:15:08,200 --> 00:15:11,078
Actually, I was having dinner
with some friends of mine.

201
00:15:12,480 --> 00:15:15,677
Mr. Matlock, if there is another time,
I beg you:

202
00:15:15,840 --> 00:15:19,230
Please let me choose the restaurant.

203
00:15:19,920 --> 00:15:22,718
Yeah. Well,
this does look about right.

204
00:15:23,600 --> 00:15:26,910
Thank you. Bye-bye.

205
00:15:30,960 --> 00:15:33,793
Good thing he stopped when he did.

206
00:15:34,360 --> 00:15:35,634
- How'd you like it?
- Good.

207
00:15:35,800 --> 00:15:37,870
I didn't think that pƒt‚ was too dry,
did you?

208
00:15:38,040 --> 00:15:40,235
How about some Italian
for the main course?

209
00:15:42,720 --> 00:15:46,918
- How is it? Al dente?
- Very al dente, but good.

210
00:15:48,960 --> 00:15:50,712
How is the new place you opened,
Mr. Leone?

211
00:15:50,880 --> 00:15:54,031
- I never got down there.
- It's much better than here.

212
00:15:54,200 --> 00:15:55,758
I suppose you know your
Victoria Edwards

213
00:15:55,920 --> 00:15:57,239
put me out of business.

214
00:15:57,400 --> 00:15:58,958
She didn't say that.

215
00:15:59,120 --> 00:16:02,351
She did say that you
solicited her opinion,

216
00:16:02,520 --> 00:16:04,750
and when she gave you a bad review,
you got very upset.

217
00:16:04,920 --> 00:16:07,559
Upset? I'm a modern chef.

218
00:16:07,720 --> 00:16:11,952
She's so old school, I doubt she even
knows how to spell ''nouvelle cuisine.''

219
00:16:12,120 --> 00:16:14,873
If my restaurant had time
to catch on,

220
00:16:15,040 --> 00:16:18,510
she and her heavy sauce-laden
recipes would be out in the cold.

221
00:16:18,680 --> 00:16:20,238
That's why she put me under.

222
00:16:20,920 --> 00:16:23,388
- Big loss?
- Just a million dollars.

223
00:16:23,840 --> 00:16:26,752
You must understand, Mr. Matlock,
people like us,

224
00:16:26,920 --> 00:16:29,514
food isn't an occupation,
it's a passion.

225
00:16:29,680 --> 00:16:33,036
Do you make it a habit of attending
the tapings of Victoria's shows?

226
00:16:33,200 --> 00:16:36,112
I wanted Michael Edwards
to come here for dinner.

227
00:16:36,280 --> 00:16:39,238
- Anything else? Some zabaglione?
- No, thanks.

228
00:16:39,880 --> 00:16:42,474
Where were you the night
before the murder, around 1 1 :00?

229
00:16:46,360 --> 00:16:49,716
- Why do you ask?
- Just say more than curiosity.

230
00:16:50,960 --> 00:16:53,394
I can always subpoena you,
if you'd prefer.

231
00:16:53,560 --> 00:16:54,993
Well, that's not necessary.

232
00:16:56,280 --> 00:16:59,875
I was here going over my receipts,
as I do every night.

233
00:17:00,040 --> 00:17:02,508
- Alone?
- Yeah.

234
00:17:03,120 --> 00:17:05,554
- Anything else?
- Maybe the cheque.

235
00:17:05,720 --> 00:17:07,995
Lunch is on the house.

236
00:17:08,880 --> 00:17:12,555
Thank you. Well, that's more like it.

237
00:17:12,720 --> 00:17:15,109
Wanna go someplace for dessert?

238
00:17:15,920 --> 00:17:17,876
My bakery has sponsored
her cooking show

239
00:17:18,040 --> 00:17:20,190
since the day it went on the air,
Mr. Matlock.

240
00:17:20,360 --> 00:17:22,032
It must have been very distressing
to you

241
00:17:22,200 --> 00:17:23,872
when she threatened to drop you.

242
00:17:24,040 --> 00:17:27,919
I cannot tell you how hurt I was.
It would be a terrible injustice.

243
00:17:28,080 --> 00:17:31,072
According to her,
your quality's gone downhill.

244
00:17:31,240 --> 00:17:32,958
I know what she's been saying,

245
00:17:33,120 --> 00:17:35,509
that we are slipping preservatives
into our pies.

246
00:17:35,680 --> 00:17:39,389
- Using nonfat milk, carob powder.
- Margarine in the pastries.

247
00:17:39,560 --> 00:17:40,993
Not a word of truth in it.

248
00:17:41,160 --> 00:17:44,596
Maybe she caught you on a day when
you were temporarily out of things.

249
00:17:44,760 --> 00:17:47,593
This isn't just a business, Mr. Matlock.
This is my life.

250
00:17:47,760 --> 00:17:51,070
I'm guaranteeing my public,
my special sweet-tooth public,

251
00:17:51,240 --> 00:17:53,834
that my pastries will be
of the consistent high quality

252
00:17:54,000 --> 00:17:56,719
they expect of Stephen Lichtman.

253
00:17:56,880 --> 00:17:59,189
Tell me this, Mr. Lichtman:

254
00:17:59,400 --> 00:18:03,234
Where were you the night before
Michael Edwards was poisoned,

255
00:18:03,400 --> 00:18:05,152
around 1 1 ?

256
00:18:05,840 --> 00:18:09,958
I was here alone
working on the wedding cake.

257
00:18:11,800 --> 00:18:13,313
Well, thank you, Mr. Lichtman.

258
00:18:13,480 --> 00:18:18,156
- May I give you something else?
- Oh, no. Not for me, thanks.

259
00:18:18,320 --> 00:18:21,312
clair? Cream puff?

260
00:18:22,320 --> 00:18:23,833
Well...

261
00:18:24,360 --> 00:18:25,839
Give him one for his cat.

262
00:18:55,520 --> 00:18:59,911
Yeah, hi. I'm Charlene Matlock,
one of Victoria's attorneys.

263
00:19:00,080 --> 00:19:03,038
- May I come in?
- Oh, certainly, certainly. Come in.

264
00:19:03,200 --> 00:19:04,872
Oh, dear, I've got a casserole
in the oven.

265
00:19:05,040 --> 00:19:07,110
Will you excuse me for a minute?

266
00:19:11,520 --> 00:19:13,511
Where'd they find the poison?

267
00:19:13,680 --> 00:19:17,389
Oh, that shelf to the left of the sink
over there.

268
00:19:17,560 --> 00:19:18,629
May I?

269
00:19:18,800 --> 00:19:21,519
Sure. You're gonna need a footstool,
though. It's on the top shelf.

270
00:19:21,680 --> 00:19:23,511
Thanks.

271
00:19:27,800 --> 00:19:29,597
I don't know how that poison
got up there.

272
00:19:29,760 --> 00:19:32,752
I can tell you one thing for sure:
It wasn't there last week.

273
00:19:33,840 --> 00:19:36,035
Did either of you have guests
that could have brought it

274
00:19:36,200 --> 00:19:37,189
and planted it up here?

275
00:19:37,760 --> 00:19:42,390
No. Nobody's been in the house
besides us for ages.

276
00:19:43,400 --> 00:19:47,837
I hated telling the police that,
but it's the truth.

277
00:19:53,560 --> 00:19:55,152
Well,

278
00:19:55,560 --> 00:19:57,312
what about the man
who just left here?

279
00:19:57,720 --> 00:19:59,597
What are you talking about?
What man?

280
00:19:59,760 --> 00:20:01,512
The man I saw running out the back.

281
00:20:01,680 --> 00:20:04,717
- Oh, you're mistaken.
- I don't think so.

282
00:20:05,720 --> 00:20:09,076
- I could track him down and ask him.
- All right, all right, all right.

283
00:20:10,000 --> 00:20:13,549
He's a friend. A good friend.

284
00:20:14,880 --> 00:20:16,552
A married friend.

285
00:20:19,960 --> 00:20:22,758
Well, how often did you see him
last week?

286
00:20:23,840 --> 00:20:26,752
Every day. He's a neighbour.
I mean, we go out to lunch

287
00:20:26,920 --> 00:20:29,195
or to the movies or--

288
00:20:29,360 --> 00:20:31,828
Somebody could have come in
while you were out with your friend

289
00:20:32,000 --> 00:20:33,956
and planted the poison?

290
00:20:34,120 --> 00:20:36,429
Yes, but I'm not gonna say that
on the stand.

291
00:20:36,600 --> 00:20:40,275
If this got out, it could ruin him.
I'm not gonna do that to him.

292
00:20:43,360 --> 00:20:46,079
Boy, that took all afternoon,
but it was worth it.

293
00:20:46,240 --> 00:20:47,355
Here you go, Chuck.

294
00:20:47,520 --> 00:20:50,910
And when you take a guy to lunch,
you take a guy to lunch.

295
00:20:51,080 --> 00:20:52,672
You know what the old folks
used to say?

296
00:20:52,840 --> 00:20:55,479
- What's that?
- Them good eats.

297
00:20:56,040 --> 00:21:01,592
Now, Frank, on this tape,
see if there's anything in the voice,

298
00:21:01,760 --> 00:21:05,275
the tone, a phrase,
anything from these three men

299
00:21:05,440 --> 00:21:07,954
that sounds like the anonymous tip
you got on the phone.

300
00:21:08,760 --> 00:21:11,797
Go ahead, Chuck.
And Chuck, put a phone filter on it.

301
00:21:11,960 --> 00:21:14,758
<i>The vinaigrette too oily,</i>
<i>the butter rock-hard,</i>

302
00:21:14,920 --> 00:21:16,512
<i>and there is enough sand</i>
<i>in these oysters</i>

303
00:21:16,680 --> 00:21:18,477
<i>to declare this beachfront property.</i>

304
00:21:18,640 --> 00:21:20,915
- No.
- Next one.

305
00:21:22,080 --> 00:21:23,399
<i>I'm a modern chef.</i>

306
00:21:23,840 --> 00:21:28,960
<i>She's so old school, I doubt she even</i>
<i>knows how to spell ''nouvelle cuisine. ''</i>

307
00:21:29,760 --> 00:21:30,988
Go to the baker, Chuck.

308
00:21:34,040 --> 00:21:37,237
<i>It's not true.</i>
<i>I know what she's been saying,</i>

309
00:21:37,400 --> 00:21:40,198
<i>that we are slipping preservatives</i>
<i>into our pies.</i>

310
00:21:42,120 --> 00:21:43,633
Let me hear Leone again.

311
00:21:47,720 --> 00:21:49,278
<i>I'm a modern chef.</i>

312
00:21:49,440 --> 00:21:54,639
<i>She's so old school, I doubt she even</i>
<i>knows how to spell ''nouvelle cuisine. ''</i>

313
00:21:55,200 --> 00:21:57,998
- That could be him.
- Thanks, Chuck.

314
00:21:58,160 --> 00:21:59,195
Now, wait a minute, Ben.

315
00:21:59,360 --> 00:22:01,954
I said that could be him,
not was him.

316
00:22:02,120 --> 00:22:04,031
That's not a positive ID.

317
00:22:05,280 --> 00:22:06,474
It's a start.

318
00:22:07,000 --> 00:22:08,194
It's a start.

319
00:22:40,240 --> 00:22:42,151
Mr. Leone?

320
00:23:50,240 --> 00:23:53,038
All right. He's here. Let's eat.

321
00:23:53,960 --> 00:23:58,033
Come on. Max, Max, Max.
Stay. Stay, stay, stay.

322
00:23:58,200 --> 00:24:01,829
That's a boy. That's a boy.

323
00:24:02,520 --> 00:24:06,479
I made roast pork, mashed potatoes,
greens, gravy, just for you.

324
00:24:06,640 --> 00:24:09,074
Oh, thanks. I'm still trying to
get over yesterday.

325
00:24:09,240 --> 00:24:11,276
- You're not gonna eat anything?
- I don't think so.

326
00:24:11,440 --> 00:24:15,069
- You said you didn't eat much.
- I didn't. I got full watching Frank eat.

327
00:24:15,240 --> 00:24:18,118
Well, that's your loss. Victoria,
set that platter down right over here.

328
00:24:18,280 --> 00:24:21,716
- Charlene, mashed potatoes?
- No. Food groups: whole grain.

329
00:24:21,880 --> 00:24:25,236
Yeah? Well, I'm into groups too,
and I'll have a group of those potatoes.

330
00:24:25,600 --> 00:24:28,114
- What did you find out downtown?
- Not much.

331
00:24:28,280 --> 00:24:29,998
Police don't have a line
on Leone's killer?

332
00:24:30,160 --> 00:24:31,229
Not yet.

333
00:24:31,400 --> 00:24:34,710
All I know is I went over there
last night to have a talk with him,

334
00:24:34,880 --> 00:24:37,872
and when I arrived, he was sitting
with his face in a plate of spaghetti.

335
00:24:38,040 --> 00:24:41,077
Tyler, pass that pork.
I believe I will have a little taste.

336
00:24:41,240 --> 00:24:43,196
All right.

337
00:24:44,600 --> 00:24:48,195
Anyway, I think it was Leone
that tipped Lieutenant Daniels

338
00:24:48,360 --> 00:24:50,430
where the poison was in your house,

339
00:24:50,600 --> 00:24:53,114
and I think he had a partner
in the murder of your ex.

340
00:24:53,640 --> 00:24:55,437
- Well, who's the partner?
- I don't know.

341
00:24:56,120 --> 00:24:59,271
We'll have to see if Stephen Lichtman
and Christopher Hoyt

342
00:24:59,440 --> 00:25:01,670
can account for their time last night.

343
00:25:01,840 --> 00:25:03,478
I'll talk to Hoyt in the morning.

344
00:25:03,640 --> 00:25:05,915
Tyler, why don't you snoop around
Lichtman's bakery?

345
00:25:06,080 --> 00:25:07,274
Pass the greens.

346
00:25:11,920 --> 00:25:13,990
You know what the old folks
used to say?

347
00:25:14,160 --> 00:25:15,354
Them some eats.

348
00:25:26,000 --> 00:25:29,515
The autopsy was performed on
Mr. Edwards the ninth of this month.

349
00:25:29,680 --> 00:25:33,036
According to the medical examiner,
what was the cause of death?

350
00:25:33,200 --> 00:25:35,998
- A toxic dose of sodium cyanide.
- In other words, he was poisoned.

351
00:25:36,160 --> 00:25:37,354
Yes, he was.

352
00:25:38,000 --> 00:25:39,479
After receiving these findings,

353
00:25:39,640 --> 00:25:42,200
did you then search the
Victoria Edwards School of Cooking?

354
00:25:42,360 --> 00:25:44,715
- Yes, we did.
- Did you find any sodium cyanide?

355
00:25:44,880 --> 00:25:46,632
Yes, we did.
The police lab technicians

356
00:25:46,800 --> 00:25:49,394
found a substantial amount
of sodium cyanide

357
00:25:49,560 --> 00:25:53,075
in the remnants
of the <i>blanquette de veau</i>

358
00:25:53,240 --> 00:25:55,356
that Mrs. Edwards
had cooked on her show

359
00:25:55,520 --> 00:25:59,798
and served to the decedent,
which is how he ingested the poison.

360
00:25:59,960 --> 00:26:02,315
Did you then search
Miss Edwards' home?

361
00:26:02,480 --> 00:26:04,152
Yes, we did.

362
00:26:06,520 --> 00:26:11,674
I show you People's Exhibit 3
and ask if you recognise it.

363
00:26:12,400 --> 00:26:15,631
Yes, I do.
It has my mark on it and my initials.

364
00:26:15,800 --> 00:26:19,236
- Then you know what's in it.
- Sodium cyanide.

365
00:26:19,400 --> 00:26:21,311
Where was it found?

366
00:26:21,480 --> 00:26:23,710
In the kitchen
of Mrs. Edwards' home.

367
00:26:23,880 --> 00:26:26,394
- No further questions.
- Cross-examine?

368
00:26:26,560 --> 00:26:28,152
No questions, Your Honour.

369
00:26:28,320 --> 00:26:29,469
You may step down.

370
00:26:30,800 --> 00:26:34,395
Why didn't you ask him
about Peter Leone's murder?

371
00:26:34,560 --> 00:26:37,518
Well, it-- It's not relevant yet.

372
00:27:21,920 --> 00:27:24,036
What are you doing back here?

373
00:27:25,240 --> 00:27:27,595
- You see, I'm--
- You're supposed to report in up front.

374
00:27:27,760 --> 00:27:30,069
- I didn't know that.
- You're not supposed to be back here

375
00:27:30,240 --> 00:27:33,789
without wearing one of these.
Now, put it on, I'll get you started.

376
00:27:34,560 --> 00:27:36,118
You're here about the job,
aren't you?

377
00:27:36,280 --> 00:27:37,918
Yeah, yeah, you know, of course.

378
00:27:38,080 --> 00:27:41,356
But look, I just don't have
that much experience, and l--

379
00:27:41,520 --> 00:27:43,192
- Have you eaten pastry before?
- Yes.

380
00:27:43,360 --> 00:27:45,316
Then you're experienced.

381
00:27:45,480 --> 00:27:47,835
Okay, your work area's over here.

382
00:27:48,000 --> 00:27:49,433
You taste one from each tray,

383
00:27:49,600 --> 00:27:51,716
then you work your way
through all these racks.

384
00:27:51,880 --> 00:27:53,996
As we bake them,
we bring them to you.

385
00:27:54,160 --> 00:27:55,832
Okay, sit here.

386
00:27:56,000 --> 00:27:58,150
This is for discards.

387
00:27:59,840 --> 00:28:01,990
Now, eat.

388
00:28:02,160 --> 00:28:03,195
Eat?

389
00:28:03,360 --> 00:28:06,318
You are Miss Edwards'
live-in housekeeper.

390
00:28:06,480 --> 00:28:08,357
Yes. Yes, I am.

391
00:28:08,520 --> 00:28:13,799
Now, we've heard testimony that
shortly after Michael Edwards' death,

392
00:28:13,960 --> 00:28:17,430
this vial of sodium cyanide
was found in the kitchen.

393
00:28:17,600 --> 00:28:21,229
Prosecution contends that it was
put there by Victoria Edwards.

394
00:28:21,400 --> 00:28:23,595
- I don't suppose you put it there.
- No, I didn't.

395
00:28:25,720 --> 00:28:28,109
Now, I understand
that for some time,

396
00:28:28,280 --> 00:28:32,398
you've been involved
in some community-service work.

397
00:28:34,120 --> 00:28:37,715
Kind of on the side,
in your spare time.

398
00:28:39,200 --> 00:28:42,078
Yes. Yes, that's right, I have.

399
00:28:42,240 --> 00:28:43,275
And you were doing this

400
00:28:43,440 --> 00:28:46,591
prior to and just after
Michael Edwards' murder.

401
00:28:46,760 --> 00:28:47,749
Yes.

402
00:28:49,680 --> 00:28:53,958
Do these activities
cause you to go out of the house?

403
00:28:54,520 --> 00:28:57,239
- Yes.
- How often?

404
00:28:57,600 --> 00:28:59,113
Practically every day.

405
00:28:59,280 --> 00:29:03,159
Were you in the habit
of locking Miss Edwards' house

406
00:29:03,320 --> 00:29:04,958
when you were out?

407
00:29:05,120 --> 00:29:06,519
No, I'm afraid I wasn't.

408
00:29:06,680 --> 00:29:08,750
So someone could have
slipped in and out of the house

409
00:29:08,920 --> 00:29:11,275
without your knowing it.

410
00:29:11,440 --> 00:29:12,509
Yes.

411
00:29:12,680 --> 00:29:15,752
Now, I understand
that you were present

412
00:29:15,920 --> 00:29:19,071
when the police found this vial.

413
00:29:19,240 --> 00:29:23,677
- Is that-- Is that correct?
- Yes, yes, that's correct. I was.

414
00:29:23,840 --> 00:29:29,119
Would you explain to the court exactly
where the vial was found, please?

415
00:29:29,280 --> 00:29:34,434
Oh, it was in the top shelf of the
cabinet just to the left of the sink.

416
00:29:34,600 --> 00:29:36,397
Isn't that an inconvenient place?

417
00:29:37,920 --> 00:29:40,275
You have to use a footstool
to get up there.

418
00:29:41,720 --> 00:29:44,188
So whenever Miss Edwards

419
00:29:44,360 --> 00:29:48,558
wanted to get something
from that shelf, she used a footstool?

420
00:29:48,720 --> 00:29:51,518
Well, actually,
she'd get me to do it for her.

421
00:29:55,120 --> 00:29:58,112
Miss Edwards has a fear of heights,
doesn't she?

422
00:29:58,280 --> 00:29:59,759
Oh, yes.

423
00:30:00,800 --> 00:30:03,917
So you're saying that this vial

424
00:30:04,080 --> 00:30:08,232
was on a shelf that you have to
use a footstool to get to,

425
00:30:08,400 --> 00:30:12,029
in a place where Miss Edwards
would never have put it herself.

426
00:30:12,200 --> 00:30:14,873
- Yes, that's right.
- Objection.

427
00:30:15,040 --> 00:30:17,395
- Calls for conclusion.
- Sustained.

428
00:30:17,560 --> 00:30:20,233
I'm sorry, Your Honour.
No further questions.

429
00:30:20,400 --> 00:30:24,234
- Mr. Burgess?
- May I have a moment, Your Honour?

430
00:30:26,800 --> 00:30:29,268
- I spoke with Hoyt.
- Was he cooperative?

431
00:30:29,440 --> 00:30:31,396
He was surly, as a matter of fact.

432
00:30:31,560 --> 00:30:34,950
But he did say
he was at home last night, alone.

433
00:30:35,400 --> 00:30:38,119
Could he have killed Peter Leone?

434
00:30:38,640 --> 00:30:40,153
Maybe.

435
00:30:46,800 --> 00:30:50,110
Okay, buddy, the fun's over.
Time to go home.

436
00:31:04,880 --> 00:31:06,791
Oh, man.

437
00:33:00,600 --> 00:33:02,716
Still hungry, buddy?

438
00:33:03,320 --> 00:33:06,471
- What's happening in there?
- Just a few of Mr. Lichtman's friends.

439
00:33:06,640 --> 00:33:08,437
Coffee and cakes.

440
00:33:08,600 --> 00:33:10,352
With their own keys?

441
00:33:24,320 --> 00:33:26,151
What do you want?

442
00:33:31,640 --> 00:33:34,837
- What's happening?
- You are.

443
00:33:56,000 --> 00:33:57,718
- Good morning.
- Morning.

444
00:33:57,880 --> 00:33:58,869
Where's Charlene?

445
00:33:59,040 --> 00:34:01,395
At her nutritionist.
Now he thinks she's allergic to eggs.

446
00:34:01,560 --> 00:34:03,630
Pretty soon, she'll be down
to ice water and crackers.

447
00:34:03,800 --> 00:34:07,110
- What's the news?
- Have I got news.

448
00:34:07,280 --> 00:34:08,793
The bakery, right?

449
00:34:08,960 --> 00:34:12,316
There I am, snooping around
the bakery, and I get caught.

450
00:34:12,480 --> 00:34:16,758
But the guy that caught me thinks
that I'm there for a job as a taster.

451
00:34:16,920 --> 00:34:18,797
So I taste and I taste

452
00:34:18,960 --> 00:34:23,431
until my blood-sugar level is reaching
dangerously high levels, okay?

453
00:34:23,600 --> 00:34:26,592
Undaunted, at the closing bell,
I begin to snoop some more,

454
00:34:26,760 --> 00:34:29,479
and I get caught again, right?

455
00:34:29,640 --> 00:34:33,076
So I go outside and I'm going
to do some exterior snooping,

456
00:34:33,240 --> 00:34:35,390
and I get caught again.

457
00:34:35,560 --> 00:34:38,472
Now, was Tyler Hudson
daunted by this, you ask yourself?

458
00:34:38,640 --> 00:34:40,153
No.

459
00:34:40,320 --> 00:34:42,231
Now I get thrown
back inside the bakery

460
00:34:42,400 --> 00:34:44,994
and surrounded
by some very lethal-Iooking men.

461
00:34:45,160 --> 00:34:47,276
- And then I see it.
- What?

462
00:34:47,440 --> 00:34:48,668
A card table.

463
00:34:48,840 --> 00:34:52,833
- These men were there to play poker.
- Pok--?

464
00:34:53,000 --> 00:34:54,752
And that's how I got the news.

465
00:34:54,920 --> 00:34:57,070
I convince them
that I am a compulsive gambler

466
00:34:57,240 --> 00:34:58,229
and an easy mark.

467
00:34:58,400 --> 00:35:00,595
I show them my $2,000
from the chilli cook-off

468
00:35:00,760 --> 00:35:02,478
and am immediately dealt in.

469
00:35:02,640 --> 00:35:04,949
This brings me
to the three salient points.

470
00:35:05,120 --> 00:35:07,190
One, Stephen Lichtman had an alibi

471
00:35:07,360 --> 00:35:10,352
for the night Peter Leone
was murdered: poker game.

472
00:35:10,520 --> 00:35:12,795
Two, Stephen Lichtman
had two partners

473
00:35:12,960 --> 00:35:14,996
that he was gonna open up
a new restaurant with.

474
00:35:15,160 --> 00:35:16,957
Peter Leone and Christopher Hoyt.

475
00:35:17,720 --> 00:35:20,154
- What's point number three?
- I don't know.

476
00:35:20,320 --> 00:35:22,754
Point number three
is they're not very good poker players,

477
00:35:22,920 --> 00:35:27,277
because my $2,000 is now $20,000.

478
00:35:27,440 --> 00:35:29,078
Twenty thousand dollars?

479
00:35:30,480 --> 00:35:31,629
Twenty thousand.

480
00:35:32,440 --> 00:35:37,639
So Christopher Hoyt has an alibi
for the night of the break-in,

481
00:35:37,800 --> 00:35:40,837
no alibi for the night
of Peter Leone's murder.

482
00:35:41,000 --> 00:35:44,151
Stephen Lichtman,
no alibi for the night of the break-in,

483
00:35:44,320 --> 00:35:46,959
has one for the night of the murder.

484
00:35:47,600 --> 00:35:50,114
They're in business together,
all right.

485
00:35:51,080 --> 00:35:52,433
So, what do we do now?

486
00:35:53,440 --> 00:35:57,319
If we can just figure out
how the poison got in the stew.

487
00:36:07,200 --> 00:36:08,189
Charlene.

488
00:36:09,560 --> 00:36:11,790
- Charlene?
- Eggs.

489
00:36:12,880 --> 00:36:13,915
Eggs?

490
00:36:15,440 --> 00:36:16,873
Eggs.

491
00:36:20,240 --> 00:36:22,117
See? Two eggs.

492
00:36:22,280 --> 00:36:26,159
I ordered six the day before
to be delivered along with the veal.

493
00:36:26,320 --> 00:36:29,596
- I only used four.
- Why's that?

494
00:36:30,080 --> 00:36:32,071
The recipe requires two eggs.

495
00:36:32,240 --> 00:36:35,357
And I initially intended
to make it in three stages,

496
00:36:35,520 --> 00:36:36,669
which would require six eggs.

497
00:36:36,840 --> 00:36:38,512
But of course the producer
came and told me

498
00:36:38,680 --> 00:36:40,113
that Michael was gonna
be on the show

499
00:36:40,280 --> 00:36:42,396
and that I would have to give him
all this extra time.

500
00:36:42,560 --> 00:36:45,950
So I decided to make the <i>blanquette</i>
in two stages instead,

501
00:36:46,120 --> 00:36:49,510
and I only used four eggs.

502
00:36:55,040 --> 00:36:57,998
Are you sure these are the eggs

503
00:36:58,160 --> 00:37:00,720
that were in the refrigerator
the night before the show?

504
00:37:00,880 --> 00:37:02,632
Oh, they have to be.

505
00:37:03,320 --> 00:37:06,118
I mean, the school has been closed
since the day Michael died.

506
00:37:06,280 --> 00:37:10,159
We're the only ones that have
been in here except for the police.

507
00:37:14,600 --> 00:37:18,309
Your Honour, as discussed earlier
in chambers and now for the record,

508
00:37:18,480 --> 00:37:20,755
I maintain my objection
to Mr. Matlock's latest piece

509
00:37:20,920 --> 00:37:22,114
of courtroom histrionics.

510
00:37:22,280 --> 00:37:27,354
Your Honour, prosecution
has masterfully established

511
00:37:27,520 --> 00:37:30,034
that Michael Edwards was killed
by the poison in the veal stew

512
00:37:30,200 --> 00:37:31,189
prepared by my client.

513
00:37:31,360 --> 00:37:32,759
It's essential to our case

514
00:37:32,920 --> 00:37:35,992
that we establish
exactly how the poison got there.

515
00:37:36,160 --> 00:37:37,593
Objection overruled.

516
00:37:37,760 --> 00:37:41,116
I do trust, however, this won't take
too much of the court's time.

517
00:37:41,280 --> 00:37:42,269
No, Your Honour.

518
00:37:42,440 --> 00:37:43,953
As soon as Miss Edwards
gets organised,

519
00:37:44,120 --> 00:37:46,953
I'll call my next witness,
Mr. Christopher Hoyt.

520
00:37:47,120 --> 00:37:50,954
I understand you're a food critic,
Mr. Hoyt.

521
00:37:51,120 --> 00:37:53,350
Yes, for the <i>Atlanta Examiner.</i>

522
00:37:53,520 --> 00:37:55,829
Given the particulars,
I bet you followed this case

523
00:37:56,000 --> 00:37:58,230
with a great deal of interest.

524
00:37:58,400 --> 00:38:00,197
Yeah, I find it interesting, yes.

525
00:38:00,360 --> 00:38:04,672
It's not every day that French cuisine
is used as a murder weapon.

526
00:38:05,400 --> 00:38:06,879
Of course, you're aware

527
00:38:07,040 --> 00:38:09,554
it wasn't the veal stew
that killed Michael Edwards.

528
00:38:09,720 --> 00:38:12,075
It was something
that was put into the stew.

529
00:38:12,600 --> 00:38:13,749
Well, of course.

530
00:38:13,920 --> 00:38:18,630
And so far, nobody has been able to
figure out how it was put into the stew.

531
00:38:20,320 --> 00:38:21,514
Well, I'm aware of that too.

532
00:38:21,680 --> 00:38:25,958
I mean, Miss Edwards couldn't just
have dumped sodium cyanide in there

533
00:38:26,120 --> 00:38:28,475
in full view of an audience
any more than she could do it

534
00:38:28,640 --> 00:38:30,551
in this courtroom. Right, Mr. Hoyt?

535
00:38:31,280 --> 00:38:32,633
Well, I suppose.

536
00:38:33,120 --> 00:38:37,511
So the poison had to be in
one of the ingredients she was using.

537
00:38:37,680 --> 00:38:39,671
Objection. Calls for conclusion.

538
00:38:39,840 --> 00:38:40,989
Sustained.

539
00:38:41,760 --> 00:38:44,354
I think I know.

540
00:38:45,200 --> 00:38:50,718
But once again, to try to discover
what ingredient the poison was in,

541
00:38:50,880 --> 00:38:53,110
I've asked Miss Edwards
to make another...

542
00:38:53,280 --> 00:38:56,317
<i>- Blanquette de veau.</i>
- Yeah, or veal stew.

543
00:38:56,480 --> 00:38:58,038
Now, the veal and the cream
are fresh.

544
00:38:58,200 --> 00:39:00,634
All the other ingredients
came from cooking school

545
00:39:00,800 --> 00:39:03,598
and have been examined
by the police.

546
00:39:06,600 --> 00:39:08,989
Might even let you taste it.

547
00:39:09,160 --> 00:39:10,673
Give us a critique.

548
00:39:11,720 --> 00:39:16,032
As a food expert, can you tell us
what Miss Edwards is doing now?

549
00:39:16,480 --> 00:39:17,959
Of course.

550
00:39:38,760 --> 00:39:43,515
She is preparing the cream sauce
that goes atop the veal.

551
00:39:43,680 --> 00:39:45,875
Yeah, yeah.

552
00:39:46,040 --> 00:39:50,591
Oh, as a food critic,
do you know Miss Edwards?

553
00:39:50,760 --> 00:39:51,749
Of course.

554
00:39:52,680 --> 00:39:53,954
Do you like Miss Edwards?

555
00:39:55,000 --> 00:39:58,470
I am indifferent to Miss Edwards.

556
00:40:01,320 --> 00:40:05,233
Oh, excuse me, Mr. Hoyt.
The stew looks about ready.

557
00:40:11,640 --> 00:40:13,517
Looks delicious.

558
00:40:15,560 --> 00:40:17,039
Go ahead.

559
00:40:18,160 --> 00:40:20,754
Oh, no, thank you. I don't want any.

560
00:40:22,480 --> 00:40:25,040
Why not? Something wrong with it?

561
00:40:26,240 --> 00:40:28,959
I just said l-- I don't want to eat any.

562
00:40:30,080 --> 00:40:32,196
Why not, Mr. Hoyt?

563
00:40:32,520 --> 00:40:33,953
Why won't you taste it?

564
00:40:37,560 --> 00:40:38,788
You think it's poison?

565
00:40:41,000 --> 00:40:45,039
As I told you before,
the veal and the cream are fresh.

566
00:40:45,200 --> 00:40:48,556
All the other ingredients
are from the cooking school.

567
00:40:50,360 --> 00:40:51,554
Won't you taste it?

568
00:40:58,800 --> 00:41:02,315
Well, can I have another volunteer?
Somebody from the audience?

569
00:41:03,760 --> 00:41:04,875
Mr. Lichtman.

570
00:41:08,000 --> 00:41:09,672
Anybody?

571
00:41:11,960 --> 00:41:13,473
All right.

572
00:41:16,080 --> 00:41:18,071
I'll try it myself.

573
00:41:20,000 --> 00:41:21,319
Don't.

574
00:41:24,600 --> 00:41:26,033
Thank you.

575
00:41:29,200 --> 00:41:31,270
What's wrong with the stew?

576
00:41:34,840 --> 00:41:36,114
The eggs.

577
00:41:39,920 --> 00:41:42,480
The eggs have been injected
with poison.

578
00:41:43,880 --> 00:41:45,199
Say again, please.

579
00:41:48,560 --> 00:41:52,997
The eggs have been injected
with poison.

580
00:42:00,640 --> 00:42:02,232
The eggs.

581
00:42:04,120 --> 00:42:09,148
The only ingredient
the police couldn't examine.

582
00:42:10,720 --> 00:42:14,713
Because once an egg
has been used,

583
00:42:14,920 --> 00:42:20,358
and the shell thrown into the disposal,
there's nothing to examine.

584
00:42:22,320 --> 00:42:26,916
But only whoever
broke into the cooking school

585
00:42:27,080 --> 00:42:29,310
the night before the murder

586
00:42:29,480 --> 00:42:34,110
and planted the doctored eggs
could've known that.

587
00:42:37,800 --> 00:42:40,268
I didn't do it. I was with some friends.

588
00:42:40,440 --> 00:42:43,876
I know someone who will swear to it.

589
00:42:44,320 --> 00:42:45,878
I know.

590
00:42:46,680 --> 00:42:49,319
Isn't it true that Peter Leone
was an accomplice?

591
00:42:50,080 --> 00:42:52,878
And didn't he make the anonymous
phone call to the police?

592
00:42:55,040 --> 00:42:56,678
Yes.

593
00:42:56,840 --> 00:42:59,957
And after Lieutenant Daniels and I
had lunch with him,

594
00:43:00,120 --> 00:43:01,792
he was very frightened,

595
00:43:01,960 --> 00:43:03,791
and you were afraid
he had talked to us.

596
00:43:03,960 --> 00:43:06,110
- So you killed him.
- No.

597
00:43:06,280 --> 00:43:10,637
And wasn't the other accomplice
that man, Stephen Lichtman?

598
00:43:15,600 --> 00:43:19,036
Isn't it true that he's the one
who broke into the cooking school

599
00:43:19,200 --> 00:43:21,156
and substituted the injected eggs?

600
00:43:21,320 --> 00:43:24,756
And isn't it true the three of you
were going to open a new restaurant,

601
00:43:24,920 --> 00:43:27,115
but you didn't have enough faith
in each other's talent,

602
00:43:27,280 --> 00:43:30,590
and you were afraid
that Victoria Edwards could ruin you?

603
00:43:30,760 --> 00:43:35,151
So you, Leone, Lichtman
conspired to poison the stew

604
00:43:35,320 --> 00:43:37,356
in hopes of killing Victoria Edwards.

605
00:43:39,720 --> 00:43:42,359
You can't prove that.

606
00:43:42,960 --> 00:43:45,030
I don't have to prove it.

607
00:43:51,440 --> 00:43:53,078
You see,

608
00:43:53,920 --> 00:44:00,792
these are the two eggs
from the cooking school

609
00:44:01,240 --> 00:44:05,313
that were not used in the veal stew.

610
00:44:08,920 --> 00:44:11,480
You see those tiny holes?

611
00:44:14,840 --> 00:44:17,673
That's where the poison
was injected.

612
00:44:18,960 --> 00:44:22,555
So you see,
I don't have to prove anything.

613
00:44:23,280 --> 00:44:25,874
All I have to do
is show reasonable doubt

614
00:44:26,040 --> 00:44:29,794
that Victoria Edwards
killed her ex-husband.

615
00:44:31,360 --> 00:44:35,194
I leave in the capable hands
of the prosecuting attorney

616
00:44:35,360 --> 00:44:38,272
the stock, the eggs, the onions,

617
00:44:39,360 --> 00:44:40,395
and whoever.

618
00:44:44,600 --> 00:44:47,717
Lloyd, I wouldn't make an omelette
with those.

619
00:44:47,880 --> 00:44:49,029
No further questions.

620
00:44:54,360 --> 00:44:56,874
We find the defendant not guilty.

621
00:44:57,040 --> 00:44:59,190
This court is adjourned.

622
00:44:59,360 --> 00:45:01,715
- We did it.
- I, for one, enjoyed it.

623
00:45:01,880 --> 00:45:02,949
Make that two.

624
00:45:06,520 --> 00:45:08,670
Let's get out of here.

625
00:45:15,720 --> 00:45:17,676
You know, it's nearly lunch.

626
00:45:17,840 --> 00:45:21,799
Well, how about some corn bread,
country-fried steak,

627
00:45:21,960 --> 00:45:24,633
sliced tomatoes and creamed corn?

628
00:45:24,800 --> 00:45:27,633
You got any old tapes
of <i>The Lone Ranger?</i>

9999
00:00:0,500 --> 00:00:2,00
<font color="#ffff00" size=14>www.tvsubtitles.net</font>
